<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.1. The heat equation</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

    <section xml:id="subsubsec-heat-wave">
    <title>Section 2: Partial Differential Equations in the form of the Heat and Wave Equations</title>
    <subsection>
      <title>Part 2.1: Introduction to the Heat Equation in One Dimension
      </title>
      
      <introduction>
        <p>
          We'll spend some time focusing on partial differential equations and how to solve them, 
        specifically focusing on the heat and the wave equations (which use similar methods to solve).  In this worksheet we'll begin by focusing on the heat equation, and investigate 
        solutions and steady-states.
        </p>
      </introduction>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <definition xml:id="def-heat-eqn-1d">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>heat equation</term> with constant <m>\alpha</m> and heat source <m>S</m> is:
        <md>
          \ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \alpha \nabla^2 u + S
        </md>
        where <m>\ds \nabla^2 u= \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}+\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial y^2}+\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial z^2}</m> is the Laplacian of <m>u</m>.  
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>S=0</m> the equation is called <term>homogeneous</term>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In one dimension the heat equation simplifies to
        <md>
          \ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \alpha \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} + S.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the homogeneous partial differential equation with homogeneous
    boundary conditions:
    <md>
<mrow>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \amp  =  7\frac{\partial^2
        u}{\partial^2 x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>u(0, t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>u(10, t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
</md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify that <m> \ds u_1(x,t) = e^{-7\pi^2t/100}\sin\left(\frac{\pi
        x}{10}\right)</m> is a solution (this means a solution to the
    differential equation and the boundary conditions).</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Also verify that <m>\ds u_2(x,t) = e^{-28\pi^2t/100}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi
        x}{10}\right)</m> is a solution.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Do you see a pattern that can help you identify other solutions?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now verify that <m>c_1u_1(x,t) + c_2u_2(x,t)</m> is a solution for any
        constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.  This is called the <em>principle
          of superposition</em>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

    <principle xml:id="principle-superposition">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>Superposition Principal</term> states that if <m>u_1</m> and <m>u_2</m> are 
        independent solutions to a linear partial differential equation, then a linear combination
        of <m>u_1</m> and <m>u_2</m> is also a solution.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </principle>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the heat equation
    <md>
    \ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = 4\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}
    </md>
    where <m>u(x,t)</m> is the temperature in a beam of length <m>2</m> with
    boundary conditions:
    <md>
<mrow>u(0,t) \amp  =   50</mrow>
<mrow>u(2,t) \amp  =   80</mrow>
</md></p>
      <p>
        Notice the boundary conditions are not homogeneous (not equal zero).  This will actually 
        present a problem for us later if we try to solve it directly.  So 
        we need to find a workaround using something called the steady-state solution.
      </p>
      <p>A time-independent solution is called a <em>steady-state</em>
    solution <m>\ds u_{ss}</m>;  that is, <m>\ds \frac{\partial u_{ss}}{\partial t} = 0</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the steady-state solution <m>u_{ss}</m> to the heat equation with
    these boundary conditions. Hint: start from what it means to be the steady state solution and work backwards using simple antiderivatives to 
  find <m>u_ss</m>, then make sure the constants satisfy the boundary conditions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>u</m> is a solution to the
    equation and boundary conditions above and <m>u_{ss}</m> is the
    steady-state solution that you found.  Define a new function <m>v</m>
    by <m>v = u- u_{ss}</m>.  What boundary conditions are satisfied by
    <m>v</m>?  That is, what is <m>v(0,t)</m> and <m>v(2,t)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find a partial differential equation (PDE) satisfied by <m>v</m>.  You could
    do this by expressing <m>\ds \frac{\partial v}{\partial t}</m> in terms of
    <m>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t}</m> and <m>\ds \frac{\partial^2
      v}{\partial^2 x}</m> in terms of <m>\ds \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial^2
      x}</m> and using the fact that <m>u</m> satisfies a PDE.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have a beam of length <m>1</m> and
    consider the heat equation with a source
    <md>
    \ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}+x.
    </md>
    and boundary conditions:
    <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0, t)   \amp =  0</mrow>
<mrow>u(1, t)  \amp =   100.</mrow>
</md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the steady-state solution <m>u_{ss}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use the steady-state solution to form a new equation and
    boundary conditions for the function <m>v = u-u_{ss}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>




</worksheet>
